                    MLDependentTypeTheories
                    \\underoverset
                    {\underset{Cont}{\longrightarrow}}
                    {\overset{Lang}{\longleftarrow}}
                    {\simeq}
                    LocallyCartesianClosedCategories
                    \\,.
          %p
            This was originally claimed as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Seely84b"} Seely 1974 theorem 6.3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution"} substitution
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            composes strictly, while its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            is by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} very nature
            of pullbacks only defined up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %a{:href => "#Curien"} Curien
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Hofmann"} Hofmann
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            for the latter. However, the full equivalence of categories was not recovered until
            %a{:href => "#ClairambaultDybjer"} Clairambault &amp; Dybjer
            solved both problems by promoting the statement to an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalence of 2-categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CurienGarnerHofmann"} Curien, Garner &amp; Hofmann
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+identity+types"} intensional identity types
            and hence with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LumsdaineWarren13"} Lumsdaine &amp; Warren 2013
          %p We now indicate some of the details.
          %h4#TypeTheories Type theories
          %p
