                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)_{/A}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi A
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Con(T)_{/A}
                    \\simeq
                    P(T)(A)
                    \\,.
            %p
              In one direction, send a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to A
              to the dependent type
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    a : A \vdash f^{-1}(a) \coloneqq \sum_{x : X} (a = f(x))
                    \\,.
            %p
              Conversely, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a : A \vdash X(a)
              a dependent type, send it to the projection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{a : A} X(a) \to A
            %p
              One shows that this indeed gives an equivalence of categories which is compatible with base change (
              %a{:href => "#Seely"}> Seely, prop. 3.2.4
              ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              a dependent type theory and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a locally cartesian closed category, an
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretation
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a morphism of locally cartesian closed categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Con(T) \to C
                    \\,.
            %p
              An interpretation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in another dependent type theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
              is a morphism of locally cartesian closed categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Con(T) \to Con(T')
                    \\,.
          %h4#internal_language Internal language
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lang
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang(C)
              as follows
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the non-dependent types of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Lang
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang(C)
                  are the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  = succeed "-dependent" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to A
              %li
                %p
                  a context
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 1
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 : X_1 , x_2 : X_2, \cdots , x_n : X_n
                  is a tower of morphisms
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mi n
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi ⋯
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 2
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi X
                                  %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        X_n
                        \\\
                        \\downarrow
                        \\\
                        \\cdots
                        \\\
                        \\downarrow
                        \\\
                        X_2
                        \\\
                        \\downarrow
                        \\\
                        X_1
                        }
              %li
                %p
                  the terms
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo :
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t[x_A] : B[x_A]
                  are the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{/A}
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+type"} equality type
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi A
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_A = y_A)
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to A \times A
              %li
                %p …
          %h3#HomotopyTypeTheory
            Homotopy type theory and locally cartesian closed
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            All of the above has an analog in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentable
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
              has a presentation by a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+category"} type-theoretic model category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              (without, possibly, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            %p
              This includes in particular all (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"}> ∞-stack
              \-)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
              (which should in addition satisfy
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} internal logic of an (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            Some form of this statement was originally formally conjectured in (
            %a{:href => "#Joyal11"}> Joyal 11
            ), following (
            %a{:href => "#Awodey10"}> Awodey 10
            ). For more details see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "locally+cartesian+closed+%28infinity%2C1%29-category#InternalLogic"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
          %h3#HomotopyWithUnivalence
            Univalent homotopy type theory and elementary
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %blockquote
            %p
              For more details see
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} model of type theory in an (infinity,1)-topos
          %p
            A (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> locally presentable
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+Cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally Cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
            (as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HomotopyTypeTheory"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
            is an (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"}> (∞,1)-sheaf
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"}> (∞,1)-topos
            \.
          %p
            It has been conjectured in (
            %a{:href => "#Awodey10"}> Awodey 10
            ) that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
            is the categorical semantics of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"}> type of types
            ), hence that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            has categorical semantics in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            in (
            %a{:href => "#KapulkinLumsdaineVoevodsky12"}> Kapulkin-Lumsdaine-Voevodsky 12
            ), and more generally for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaf"} (∞,1)-presheaf
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elegant+Reedy+categories"} elegant Reedy categories
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman13"}> Shulman 13
            ). The general case is proven in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman19"} Shulman 19
          %p
            In these proofs the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+categories"} type-theoretic model categories
            which interpret the homotopy type theory syntax are required to provide type universes that behave strictly under pullback. This matches the usual syntactically convenient universes in type theory (either a la Russell or a la Tarski), but more difficult to implement in the categorical semantics. More flexibly, one may consider syntactic
            %a{:href => "type+of+types#TarskiStyle"} type universes weakly à la Tarski
            (
            %a{:href => "Luo12"}> Luo 12
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Gallozzi14"} Gallozzi 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+categories"} type-theoretic model categories
            presenting) any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            in this general flexible sense is in (
            %a{:href => "#GepnerKock12"}> Gepner-Kock 12
            ). For the general syntactic issue see at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/homotopytypetheory/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} model of type theory in an (infinity,1)-topos
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf"} (∞,1)-sheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            are those currently understood, the basic type theory with univalent universes does not see or care about their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} local presentability
            as such (although it is used in other places, such as the construction of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} elementary (∞,1)-topos
            such that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            and some supply of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            has categorical semantics precisely in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
            (as conjectured in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Awodey10"} Awodey 10
          %p
            Notice that this statement, once realized, makes (or would make) Univalent HoTT+HITs a sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            refinement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            as proposed by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+theory+of+the+category+of+sets"} elementary theory of the category of sets
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initiality+conjecture"} initiality conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax-semantics+duality"} syntax-semantics duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Awodey%27s+conjecture"} Awodey's conjecture
          %h2#References References
          %p
            An elementary exposition of in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              WikiBooks,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Haskell/The_Curry%E2%80%93Howard_isomorphism"} Haskell/The Curry–Howard isomorphism
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first order theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"} hyperdoctrines
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#SeelyA
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hyperdoctrines, natural deduction, and the Beck condition
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/ZML/ZML.PDF"} pdf
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            and initiality is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Simon Castellan,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Dependent type theory as the initial category with families
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://iso.mor.phis.me/archives/2011-2012/stage-2012-goteburg/report.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            which was formalized inside type theory with set quotients of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            in:
          %ul
            %li#AltKap2015
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"}> Thorsten Altenkirch
              , Ambrus Kaposi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Type Theory in Type Theory using Quotient Inductive Types
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~txa/publ/tt-in-tt.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://bitbucket.org/akaposi/tt-in-tt"} formalisation in Agda
          %p Surveys inclue
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Hirschowitz"}> Tom Hirschowitz
                ,
                %em Introduction to categorical logic
                (2010) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.lama.univ-savoie.fr/~hirschowitz/talks/cours.pdf"}> pdf
                )
              %p
                (see the discussion building up to the theorem on
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lama.univ-savoie.fr/~hirschowitz/talks/cours.pdf#page=96"} slide 96
            %li
              %p
                Roy Crole,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deriving category theory from type theory
            %li#Maietti05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maria+Maietti"}> Maria Maietti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modular correspondence between dependent type theories and categories including pretopoi and topoi
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.unipd.it/~maietti/papers/tumscs.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+categories"} star-autonomous categories
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Seely89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Linear logic,
                  = succeed "-autonomous" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories in Computer Science and Logic
              %strong 92
              (1989) &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SeelyLinearLogic.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/nets/llsac.ps.gz"} ps.gz
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/conm-92"} ISBN:978-0-8218-5100-5
          %p and reviews/further developments are in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                G. M. Bierman,
                %em What is a Categorical Model of Intuitionistic Linear Logic?
                (
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.31.8687"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                Andrew Graham Barber,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear Type Theories, Semantics and Action Calculi
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/97/ECS-LFCS-97-371/&#8206;"} web
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/97/ECS-LFCS-97-371/ECS-LFCS-97-371.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Andr%C3%A9+Melli%C3%A8s"} Paul-André Melliès
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorial Semantics of Linear Logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Interactive models of computation and program behaviour
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.pps.univ-paris-diderot.fr/~mellies/papers/panorama.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            see:
          %ul
            %li#Vakar14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthijs+V%C3%A1k%C3%A1r"}> Matthijs Vákár
                ,
                %em Syntax and Semantics of Linear Dependent Types
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1405.0033"}> arXiv:1405.0033
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#RileyFinsterLicata21
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell+Riley"}> Mitchell Riley
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Licata"} Daniel Licata
                %em Synthetic Spectra via a Monadic and Comonadic Modality
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.04099"}> arXiv:2102.04099
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell+Riley"}> Mitchell Riley
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Extending Homotopy Type Theory with Linear Type Formers
                %em The ForML Lab
                (2021) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://the-au-forml-lab.github.io/colloquium_talks/Riley.html"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sPxtdCtjSDc"} video
            %li#Riley22Thesis
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell+Riley"}> Mitchell Riley
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Bunched Homotopy Type Theory for Synthetic Stable Homotopy Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.14418/wes01.3.139"} doi:10.14418/wes01.3.139
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://digitalcollections.wesleyan.edu/object/ir%3A3269"} ir:3269
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://mvr.hosting.nyu.edu/pubs/thesis.pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (using ideas from
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunched+logic"} bunched logic
            %li#Riley22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell+Riley"}> Mitchell Riley
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear Homotopy Type Theory
                %a{:href => "https://www.uwo.ca/math/faculty/kapulkin/seminars/hottest_junior_2022.html"} HoTTEST Event for Junior Researchers 2022
                (Jan 2022) &amp;lbrack;slides:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.uwo.ca/math/faculty/kapulkin/seminars/hottestfiles/Riley-2022-01-20-HoTTEST.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o2oWhHabjdM"} YT
          %p
            The relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typed+lambda-calculus"} typed lambda-calculus
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+categories"} cartesian closed categories
            is discussed in part I, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            between the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theories
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            is discussed in chapter II of:
          %ul
            %li#LambekScott86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+J.+Scott"} Philip J. Scott
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to higher order categorical logic
              %strong 7
              (1986) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/ae/academic/subjects/mathematics/logic-categories-and-sets/introduction-higher-order-categorical-logic?format=PB&isbn=9780521356534"}> ISBN: 0-521-24665-2
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Mzk-Levi/texts/master/Lambek%20J.%2C%20Scott%20P.J.%20Introduction%20to%20Higher%20Order%20Categorical%20Logic.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+categories"} locally cartesian closed categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            was originally claimed in
          %ul
            %li#Seely84b
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally cartesian closed categories and type theory
              %strong 95
              (1984) 33-48 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100061284"}> doi:10.1017/S0305004100061284
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/LCCC/LCCC.pdf"} pdf
          %p following a statement earlier conjectured in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An intuitionistic theory of types: predicative part
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.131.926"} web
          %p The problem with strict substitution compared to weak pullback in this argument was discussed and fixed in
          %ul
            %li#Curien
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre-Louis+Curien"}> Pierre-Louis Curien
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Substitution up to isomorphism
                %strong 19
                1-2 (1993) 51-86 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/175469.175471"}> doi:10.5555/175469.175471
                \&amp;rbrack; and: Diagrammes
                %strong 23
                (1990) 43-66 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/DIA_1990__23__43_0"}> numdam:DIA_1990__23__43_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Hofmann
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the interpretation of type theory in locally cartesian closed categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computer Science Logic. CSL 1994
                %strong 933
                (1994) 427–441 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0022273"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0022273
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p but in the process the equivalence of categories was lost. This was finally all rectified in
          %ul
            %li#ClairambaultDybjer
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Clairambault"}> Pierre Clairambault
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Dybjer"} Peter Dybjer
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Biequivalence of Locally Cartesian Closed Categories and Martin-Löf Type Theories
              = succeed "," do
                %em Typed lambda calculi and applications
              %strong 6690
              Springer (2011) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3456"}> arXiv:1112.3456
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_10"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_10
          %p and
          %ul
            %li#CurienGarnerHofmann
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre-Louis+Curien"}> Pierre-Louis Curien
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"} Martin Hofmann
              = succeed "," do
                %em Revisiting the categorical interpretation of dependent type theory
              %strong 546
              21 (2014) 99-119 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2014.03.003"}> doi:10.1016/j.tcs.2014.03.003
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/CurienGarnerHofmann.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Another version of this which also applies to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+identity+types"} intensional identity types
            and hence to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#LumsdaineWarren13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"}> Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Warren"} Michael Warren
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An overlooked coherence construction for dependent type theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LumsdaineWarren2013.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"}> Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Warren"} Michael Warren
                %em The local universes model: an overlooked coherence construction for dependent type theories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1411.1736"}> arXiv:1411.1736
                )
          %p
            The analogous statement relating
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} locally cartesian closed (infinity,1)-categories
            was formally conjectured around
          %ul
            %li#Joyal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Remarks on homotopical logic
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://hottheory.files.wordpress.com/2011/06/report-11_2011.pdf#page=19"} pdf
          %p
            following earlier suggestions by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"} Steve Awodey
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            axiom added this is refined to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+theory"} (∞,1)-topos theory
            appears around
          %ul
            %li#Awodey
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              %em Type theory and homotopy
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/preprints/TTH.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Details on this higher categorical semantics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            are in
          %ul
            %li#Shulman12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"}> Michael Shulman
              ,
              %em Univalence for inverse diagrams and homotopy canonicity
              (
              %em> From type theory and homotopy theory to Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
              ), Mathematical Structures in Computer Science
              %strong 25
              5 (2015) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.3253"}> arXiv:1203.3253
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129514000565"} doi:/10.1017/S0960129514000565
          %p with lecture notes in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical models of homotopy type theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://home.sandiego.edu/~shulman/hottminicourse2012/03models.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Joyal11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Remarks on homotopical logic
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://hottheory.files.wordpress.com/2011/06/report-11_2011.pdf#page=19"} pdf
            %li#Joyal14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical homotopy type theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/homotopytypetheory/files/Joyal.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li#Kapulkin14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Type theory and locally cartesian closed quasicategories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g87bZJ2bvYk"} video
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The homotopy theory of type theories
                %strong 337
                (2018) 1-38 (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00037"}> arXiv:1610.00037
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2018.08.003"} doi:10.1016/j.aim.2018.08.003
            %li#KapulkinSzumilo17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karol+Szumilo"} Karol Szumilo
                %em
                  Internal Language of Finitely Complete
                  = succeed "-categories" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.09519"}> arXiv:1709.09519
                )
            %li#Isaev16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valery+Isaev"}> Valery Isaev
                ,
                %em Algebraic Presentations of Dependent Type Theories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.08504"}> arXiv:1602.08504
                )
            %li#Isaev18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valery+Isaev"}> Valery Isaev
                ,
                %em Morita equivalences between algebraic dependent type theories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05045"}> arXiv:1804.05045
                )
          %p
            Models specifically in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"}> constructive
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#BezemCoquandHuber13
              %p
                Marc Bezem,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A model of type theory in cubical sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2014/4628/"} web
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2014/4628/pdf/7.pdf"} pdf
            %li#KaposiAltenkirch14
              %p
                Ambrus Kaposi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"} Thorsten Altenkirch
                %em A syntax for cubical type theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://mazzo.li/dump/aim-kaposi-pres.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Docherty14
              %p
                Simon Docherty,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A model of type theory in cubical sets with connection
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.illc.uva.nl/Research/Publications/Reports/MoL-2014-12.text.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A precise definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} elementary (infinity,1)-topos
            inspired by giving a natural equivalence to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            was then proposed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              %em Inductive and higher inductive types
              (2012) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.sandiego.edu/~shulman/hottminicourse2012/04induction.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Categorical semantics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Awodey10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                ,
                %em Type theory and homotopy
                (2010) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/awodey/preprints/TTH.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#KapulkinLumsdaineVoevodsky12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
                %em The Simplicial Model of Univalent Foundations
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.2851"}> arXiv:1211.2851
